' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' mustache/lib.mfb — the package's public API.
'
' `render` and `renderWith` are the same function with and without partials;
' `escapeHtml` exposes the escaping a `{{name}}` tag applies, so a caller
' building HTML around a rendered fragment can use the same rule; and
' `limitCode` names the one failure with no `errorCode::` equivalent.
'
' Nothing here touches the filesystem: read the template and the partials
' yourself and hand over the Strings, which is the same split `csv::parse`,
' `json::parse` and `yaml::parse` use.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

IMPORT json
IMPORT collections

DOC
  PACKAGE
  DESC Render Mustache templates against a `json::Json` context.
  DESC
  DESC `mustache::render` takes a template and a `json::Json` value and returns
  DESC the rendered text; `mustache::renderWith` adds a
  DESC `Map OF String TO String` of partials. The context is an ordinary
  DESC `json::Json` tree, so anything that already produces one -- `json::parse`
  DESC on a config file, `yaml::parse` on a YAML one, an HTTP body -- is a
  DESC context. Because the model is `json::Json`, an importer writes
  DESC `IMPORT json` alongside `IMPORT mustache` to name the type.
  DESC
  DESC The package owns no filesystem access, exactly as `csv::parse` and
  DESC `json::parse` own none: read the template and pass the `String`.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT mustache
    IMPORT json
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Integer
      LET template AS String = "Dear {{name}},\n{{#overdue}}Your loan of {{title}} is overdue.\n{{/overdue}}"
      LET context AS json::Json = json::parse("{\"name\":\"Ada\",\"overdue\":{\"title\":\"The Analytical Engine\"}}")
      io::print(mustache::render(template, context))
      ' Dear Ada,
      ' Your loan of The Analytical Engine is overdue.
      RETURN 0
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
  DESC
  DESC In a program the template comes from a file, which this package does not
  DESC read for you -- `fs::readText("page.mustache")` and
  DESC `json::parse(fs::readText("page.json"))` produce both arguments.
  DESC
  DESC What it implements
  DESC
  DESC The six required modules of the Mustache specification, version 1.4:
  DESC interpolation (`{{name}}`), sections (`{{#name}}`), inverted sections
  DESC (`{{^name}}`), comments (`{{! note}}`), partials (`{{>name}}`), and
  DESC set-delimiters (`{{=<% %>=}}`). That includes the parts of the
  DESC specification that are easy to miss and impossible to work around:
  INFO Dotted names. `{{a.b.c}}` walks into nested objects. Only the FIRST
  INFO segment searches the context stack; the rest resolve strictly against
  INFO what came before, so an enclosing `b` is never a fallback for a missing
  INFO `b` inside `a`.
  INFO
  INFO The implicit iterator. `{{.}}` is the current context itself, which is
  INFO how `{{#tags}}{{.}} {{/tags}}` prints a list of strings.
  INFO
  INFO Standalone lines. A line holding nothing but whitespace and a section,
  INFO comment, partial or set-delimiters tag disappears completely -- tag,
  INFO whitespace and newline. This is what lets `{{#items}}` sit on its own
  INFO line without opening a blank line in the output. An interpolation is
  INFO content, so a line carrying one is never standalone.
  INFO
  INFO Partial indentation. A standalone `{{>body}}` reprints the partial under
  INFO the whitespace that preceded it, applied to the partial's SOURCE before
  INFO it is parsed -- so the partial's own lines are indented and the newlines
  INFO inside an interpolated value are not.
  INFO
  INFO Delimiter scope. A `{{=<% %>=}}` holds until the end of the template that
  INFO wrote it. It does not reach into a partial, and a change made inside a
  INFO partial does not leak back out.
  DESC
  DESC Deliberate limits
  DESC
  INFO Lambdas are not supported. They are the specification's one optional
  INFO module, and they ask for a context value that is a function; a
  INFO `json::Json` tree holds null, booleans, numbers, strings, arrays and
  INFO objects, and none of those is callable. A `{{#name}}` over a value that
  INFO is not a lambda behaves exactly as the specification says, so a template
  INFO written for a lambda renders as an ordinary section rather than failing.
  INFO
  INFO Escaping is the reference implementation's, not the minimum. `{{name}}`
  INFO escapes `&`, `<`, `>`, `"`, `'`, `/`, `=` and the backtick, matching
  INFO mustache.js and Handlebars character for character. That is three more
  INFO than `encoding::htmlEscape` rewrites, and it writes `&#39;` for an
  INFO apostrophe where `encoding::htmlEscape` writes `&apos;`. Use
  INFO `mustache::escapeHtml` when you need the same rule outside a template.
  INFO
  INFO Interpolating an array or an object writes its compact JSON form.
  INFO The specification says nothing about this case and implementations
  INFO answer with their host language's "to string" -- mustache.js writes
  INFO `[object Object]`. JSON is deterministic, identical on every platform,
  INFO and legible.
  INFO
  INFO Zero and the empty string are falsey, as they are in the reference
  INFO implementation, so `{{#count}}` over `0` renders nothing. The
  INFO specification does not say, and Ruby's implementation decides the other
  INFO way.
  INFO
  INFO Whitespace means ASCII whitespace. A no-break space inside a tag or on an
  INFO otherwise-standalone line is content here, where JavaScript's `\s` would
  INFO count it as whitespace.
  SEC Template size, section nesting, partial recursion and output size are all
  SEC bounded, so an untrusted template cannot exhaust memory or the stack. A
  SEC template (and each partial body) may be 1048576 bytes, sections may nest
  SEC 64 deep, partials may expand 16 levels, and the output may reach 16777216
  SEC bytes. The partial limit is the load-bearing one: a partial may name any
  SEC partial including itself, so `{{>a}}` inside partial `a` would otherwise
  SEC expand forever, and a partial that includes itself inside a section over a
  SEC list multiplies at every level. The output limit is checked as the output
  SEC grows rather than at the end, so such a template stops at the limit
  SEC instead of after exhausting memory.
  SEC
  SEC Partials are supplied as a `Map OF String TO String`, never loaded from
  SEC disk, so a template cannot reach a file the caller did not choose. A
  SEC program that does want partials from a directory reads them itself, and
  SEC should use `fs::isWithin` or `fs::openWithin` to keep a `../` in a partial
  SEC name from escaping that directory.
  DESC
  DESC Errors
  DESC
  DESC `errorCode::ErrInvalidFormat` for a malformed template,
  DESC `errorCode::ErrDepthExceeded` for section nesting or partial recursion
  DESC past the limits, and `mustache::limitCode()` (93120001) for a template or
  DESC an output past its size limit. That last one is a generator-9 code, which
  DESC is not globally unique, so match it only around a call you already know
  DESC is this package's.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC render
  GROUP Rendering
  DESC Render `template` against `context` and return the result.
  DESC
  DESC A `{{>name}}` partial renders as nothing, because this form supplies
  DESC none; use `mustache::renderWith` to provide them.
  ARG template The Mustache template. Reading a file is the caller's job: pass `fs::readText(path)`.
  ARG context The values the template's names resolve against. Any `json::Json` value is accepted; a template's names can only resolve against a `json::JsonObj`, so anything else renders every name as empty.
  RET The rendered text.
  ERROR 77050003 The template is malformed: a tag is never closed, a `{{/name}}` closes a section that was never opened or is not the innermost open one, a section is left open at the end, or a `{{= =}}` tag does not name exactly two delimiters.
  ERROR 77050024 Sections nest more than 64 levels deep.
  ERROR 93120001 The template is larger than 1048576 bytes, or the output grew past 16777216 bytes.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT mustache
    IMPORT json
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Integer
      LET context AS json::Json = json::parse("{\"name\":\"mfb\",\"tags\":[\"fast\",\"small\"]}")
      io::print(mustache::render("Hello {{name}}! {{#tags}}[{{.}}]{{/tags}}", context))
      ' Hello mfb! [fast][small]
      RETURN 0
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC
EXPORT FUNC render(template AS String, context AS json::Json) AS String
  RETURN renderSource(template, context, Map OF String TO String {})
END FUNC

DOC
  FUNC renderWith
  GROUP Rendering
  DESC Render `template` against `context`, resolving `{{>name}}` tags in
  DESC `partials`.
  DESC
  DESC A `{{>name}}` whose name is not a key of `partials` renders as nothing
  DESC rather than failing, which is what the specification requires -- a
  DESC template may name a partial that only some callers supply.
  DESC
  DESC A partial is parsed with the DEFAULT `{{` and `}}` delimiters however the
  DESC including template had changed them, and a `{{= =}}` inside a partial
  DESC does not leak back out to the template that included it.
  ARG template The Mustache template.
  ARG context The values the template's names resolve against.
  ARG partials The partial bodies, keyed by the name a `{{>name}}` tag writes. Supplying them in memory is the whole interface: this package never reads a file, so a template cannot reach one the caller did not choose.
  RET The rendered text.
  ERROR 77050003 The template, or one of the partials it expanded, is malformed.
  ERROR 77050024 Sections nest more than 64 levels deep, or partials expand more than 16 levels deep -- which is also how a partial that eventually includes itself is reported.
  ERROR 93120001 The template or a partial body is larger than 1048576 bytes, or the output grew past 16777216 bytes.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT mustache
    IMPORT json
    IMPORT collections
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Integer
      MUT partials AS Map OF String TO String = Map OF String TO String {}
      partials = collections::set(partials, "row", "  <li>{{name}}</li>\n")
      LET context AS json::Json = json::parse("{\"people\":[{\"name\":\"Ada\"},{\"name\":\"Alan\"}]}")
      io::print(mustache::renderWith("<ul>\n{{#people}}\n{{>row}}\n{{/people}}\n</ul>", context, partials))
      ' <ul>
      '   <li>Ada</li>
      '   <li>Alan</li>
      ' </ul>
      RETURN 0
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC
EXPORT FUNC renderWith(template AS String, context AS json::Json, partials AS Map OF String TO String) AS String
  RETURN renderSource(template, context, partials)
END FUNC

DOC
  FUNC escapeHtml
  GROUP Rendering
  DESC Escape text exactly as a `{{name}}` tag escapes it.
  DESC
  DESC Eight characters are rewritten: `&` to `&amp;`, `<` to `&lt;`, `>` to
  DESC `&gt;`, `"` to `&quot;`, `'` to `&#39;`, `/` to `&#x2F;`, `=` to `&#x3D;`
  DESC and the backtick to `&#x60;`. Everything else, including every non-ASCII
  DESC character, passes through unchanged, and the function never fails.
  DESC
  DESC This is not `encoding::htmlEscape`. That function escapes the five XML
  DESC metacharacters and writes an apostrophe as `&apos;`; this one matches the
  DESC Mustache reference implementation character for character, which means
  DESC `&#39;` for an apostrophe and three additional characters. `/` closes a
  DESC `<script>` block early from inside a string literal, the backtick is an
  DESC attribute delimiter in older Internet Explorer, and `=` starts an
  DESC attribute inside an unquoted attribute value -- each turns a
  DESC loosely-quoted attribute into an injection point. Escaping more than the
  DESC minimum is always safe, so this is the stronger of the two.
  DESC
  DESC It is exported so that a program assembling HTML around a rendered
  DESC fragment escapes by the same rule the template did.
  ARG text The text to escape. Any String, including the empty one.
  RET The escaped text.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT mustache
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Integer
      io::print(mustache::escapeHtml("<a href='/x'>T & J</a>"))
      ' &lt;a href&#x3D;&#39;&#x2F;x&#39;&gt;T &amp; J&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;
      RETURN 0
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC
EXPORT FUNC escapeHtml(text AS String) AS String
  RETURN escapeText(text)
END FUNC

DOC
  FUNC limitCode
  GROUP Rendering
  DESC The error code this package raises when a template or its output passes
  DESC a size limit: 93120001, written `9-312-0001`.
  DESC
  DESC It is the one failure here with no `errorCode::` equivalent -- the
  DESC registry models no "this input is larger than the reader will handle" --
  DESC so it takes a code from generator 9, the range reserved for codes a
  DESC package defines for itself. Generator-9 codes carry no registry meaning
  DESC and are not globally unique: two unrelated packages may both use this
  DESC integer for unrelated failures. Match it only around a call you already
  DESC know is this package's, never across a broad block.
  DESC
  DESC The two limits it reports are a template (or partial body) over 1048576
  DESC bytes and an output over 16777216 bytes. Recursion is reported
  DESC separately, as `errorCode::ErrDepthExceeded`.
  RET 93120001.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT mustache
    IMPORT json
    IMPORT io

    FUNC page(template AS String, context AS json::Json) AS String
      LET html AS String = mustache::render(template, context) TRAP(problem)
        IF problem.code = mustache::limitCode() THEN
          io::printError("that template renders too much output")
          RECOVER ""
        END IF
        PROPAGATE
      END TRAP
      RETURN html
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC
EXPORT FUNC limitCode() AS Integer
  RETURN ERR_LIMIT
END FUNC
